        "id": "state",
        "title": "7. Explicit task state and progress",
        "stage": "foundation",
        "takeaway": "State records what the application knows about a task: validated inputs, observed results, pending work and terminal status. Keep operational state separate from conversational prose.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Understand the idea",
            "paragraphs": [
              "State records what the application knows about a task: validated inputs, observed results, pending work and terminal status. Keep operational state separate from conversational prose.",
              "A progress claim such as I searched everything is not a database fact. Record actual search queries and accepted result IDs from the executor. Use clear statuses such as running, needs_input, completed and failed. If a task resumes later, it needs enough information to distinguish a proposed action from one that actually executed."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Work through the example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Read this as an explicit scenario or a small local example. Identify the decision, the trusted application check and the observable result before continuing."
            ],
            "example": "state = {\n    \"status\": \"running\",\n    \"queries\": [\"joins\"],\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"SQL-07\"],\n    \"remaining_steps\": 2,\n}\nassert state[\"status\"] != \"completed\"\nassert \"SQL-07\" in state[\"evidence_ids\"]"
          },
          {
            "title": "Failures and tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Appending every message forever is not a state model. It makes recovery and authorization difficult to inspect. Conversely, reducing all history to one summary may lose important pending-action details. Define which state fields are authoritative, who may update them and what is persisted."
            ]
          }
        ],
