        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Understand the idea",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Multiple agents can separate independent responsibilities, but they add coordination, context transfer, latency and failure modes. Start with a single bounded system when it meets the task.",
              "A research helper might ask independent workers to inspect Python and SQL prerequisites, then combine results. Each handoff needs a bounded question, source provenance, expected output and permissions. A reviewer agent can catch errors, but shared models and shared evidence can produce correlated mistakes; agreement is not proof."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Work through the example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Read this as an explicit scenario or a small local example. Identify the decision, the trusted application check and the observable result before continuing."
            ],
            "example": "Coordinator: compare prerequisites, no writes\n  -> Python worker: return sources + claims + uncertainties\n  -> SQL worker: return sources + claims + uncertainties\n  <- structured results, possibly conflicting\nCoordinator: reconcile evidence, cite claims, stop\nOne worker failing does not automatically validate the other."
          },
          {
            "title": "Failures and tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Avoid circular delegation and unbounded fan-out. Assign ownership for shared state and set aggregate budgets, not just per-worker limits. Parallelism may reduce wall time while increasing total cost. Measure whether the split improves outcomes enough to justify the overhead."
            ]
          }
        ],
        "exercise": {
          "prompt": "Compare one agent with two workers on the same prerequisite task. What should the experiment record?",
          "solution": "Record task correctness, source support, forbidden actions, total usage, end-to-end latency and coordination failures. Include conflict and worker-timeout cases. Keep task sets and scoring rules fixed. Choose the simpler architecture if quality gains are absent or costs outweigh them.",
          "checks": [
            "Uses identical tasks and scoring.",
            "Measures aggregate cost and failure behavior.",
            "Does not treat agreement as independent proof."
          ],
          "solutionFormat": "prose"
        },
        "quiz": {
          "question": "Does adding a reviewer agent guarantee correctness?",
          "options": [
            "No; reviewers can share blind spots and need their own evaluation",
            "Yes; two agents always make independent mistakes",
            "Yes, if the reviewer has a different name"
          ],
          "correct": 0,
          "explanation": "A second role adds another fallible judgment. Independence and useful error detection must be demonstrated, not assumed."
        },
