        "id": "binary-search",
        "title": "5. Binary search and invariants",
        "takeaway": "Discard half the candidates only when order justifies it.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Understand the idea",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Binary search requires data sorted under the same comparison used by the search. Maintain inclusive low and high indexes. If the middle value is below the target, all values at or left of it can be excluded.",
              "The invariant is: if the target exists and has not been found, it lies in the remaining range. Each update must shrink that range. This version returns any matching index, not necessarily the first duplicate."
            ],
            "example": "def binary_search(values, target):\n    low, high = 0, len(values) - 1\n    while low <= high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if values[mid] == target:\n            return mid\n        if values[mid] < target:\n            low = mid + 1\n        else:\n            high = mid - 1\n    return -1\n\nassert binary_search([2, 5, 8, 12, 16], 12) == 3"
          },
          {
            "title": "Reason about the tradeoff",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Sorting an unsorted list first has a cost. For one search, a linear scan may be simpler; binary search is valuable when sorted data already exists or is reused."
            ]
          }
        ],
        "exercise": {
          "prompt": "Trace target 6 in [2,5,8,12,16], then test empty and one-element arrays.",
          "solution": "# Using binary_search above:\nassert binary_search([2, 5, 8, 12, 16], 6) == -1\nassert binary_search([], 6) == -1\nassert binary_search([6], 6) == 0\n# Mid values: 8, 2, 5; then low > high.",
          "checks": [
            "Every iteration shrinks the candidate interval.",
            "Worst-case search time O(log n), auxiliary space O(1).",
            "Duplicates may produce any matching index."
          ]
        },
